// Signing.xcconfig — code signing settings, kept out of project.pbxproj.
//
// This file is the project's base configuration. It exists so that
// DEVELOPMENT_TEAM does not live in project.pbxproj, where it would be a
// spurious diff in every pull request from anyone who is not the maintainer.
//
// IMPORTANT: a build setting written into project.pbxproj overrides this file.
// Selecting a team in Xcode's Signing & Capabilities tab makes Xcode write
// DEVELOPMENT_TEAM into every target, which silently shadows the values below
// from then on. If `git status` shows project.pbxproj changed after you opened
// that tab, revert it.

// Optional local overrides, for anyone who is not the maintainer. The "?" makes
// a missing file a no-op rather than a build error, so a plain clone just works.
// See Signing.local.xcconfig.example.
#include? "Signing.local.xcconfig"

// The maintainer's Apple Developer Team. Harmless to publish — it is embedded in
// every signed binary anyway.
DEVELOPMENT_TEAM = $(LOCAL_DEVELOPMENT_TEAM:default=8HTCTY2VH7)

// Normal development signing, which is what a Mac with a provisioning profile
// for the team above wants: the signature is stable across builds, so macOS does
// not treat every rebuild as a new, unverified application.
//
// Without an Apple developer account, or without access to that team, this fails.
// Set LOCAL_CODE_SIGN_IDENTITY = - in Signing.local.xcconfig to sign ad-hoc
// instead; the app runs perfectly well that way.
CODE_SIGN_IDENTITY = $(LOCAL_CODE_SIGN_IDENTITY:default=Apple Development)
